<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  The workout session is what keeps the app alive with the wrist down, and
	  that is all this entitlement is for. Two others used to sit here and
	  neither was ever used: `healthkit.background-delivery`, which is for
	  observer queries waking the app on new samples, and `healthkit.access`,
	  which is for clinical records and was an empty array.

	  They are gone because an unused entitlement is not free. Every one of them
	  has to be present on the App ID and in the provisioning profile, and when
	  automatic signing cannot add one, the failure lands as an embedded watch
	  app that quietly never installs — with nothing on screen to say why.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.developer.healthkit</key>
	<true/>

	<!--
	  WeatherKit, for the wind a session gets measured against.

	  Nothing on the watch calls it yet. Today a session syncs to the phone and
	  `WeatherLookup.recordedWind` does the lookup there, replacing the wind
	  guessed from the shape of the track with the wind that was actually
	  blowing. This entitlement is here ahead of the watch doing any of that
	  itself.

	  Read the paragraph above about unused entitlements before the next
	  archive, because it applies to this one until the watch actually uses it.
	  The App ID it has to be present on is com.laan.labs.openWater.watchkitapp
	  — its own identifier, not the phone's — and WeatherKit has to be enabled
	  there on BOTH tabs, Capabilities and App Services. Capabilities alone is
	  what cost three weeks on the phone App ID: it produces a correct-looking
	  binary and profile that fails every call with

	    WeatherDaemon.WDSJWTAuthenticatorServiceListener.Errors Code=2 "(null)"

	  and the App Services half leaves nothing on this machine to check against.
	  See openWater/openWater.entitlements for the longer version.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.developer.weatherkit</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
